In 2018, Shawn Souza, a Dartmouth, MA police officer was arrested and charged with alleged sexual abuse of his step daughter. The charges included aggravated rape of a child and indecent assault and battery. A jury unanimously convicted Souza and sentenced him to  10-15 years in prison on October 3,2024. He was released pending appeal. 

In my opinion, this is another instance of judicial overreach in Massachusetts. This man has been granted a new trial by Superior Court Judge Elizabeth Sullivan who did not hear the original case. Her decision was based on improper joinder and the presence in the courtroom of three persons from a group known as “Bikers against child abuse (BACA)” in the court room. There is no evidence that these people were disruptive in any way to the proceedings or that the jury took any notice of them. 

The joinder issue involves a previous charge (different victim) which ultimately resulted in a mistrial stemming from the alleged abuse of Sousa’s step daughter when she was aged 6-8. The case resulted in a hung jury in 2022. Judge McGuire allowed the joinder after pretrial which was not objected to by the defense at the time. 

To be clear, this former police officer is accused of raping his stepdaughter when she was 6-8 years old and is also accused of molesting a different 15 year old relative. 

He has been released pending these appeals based on one judge’s opinions after a unanimous jury guilty verdict after trial.  This case will be active again over the next few months and I encourage anyone who cares about judicial accountability, first amendment rights and rights of minors to look into the details of this case. 
